WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.281
You've got a change to review,

00:00:02.281 --> 00:00:03.625
and a folder of configs.

00:00:04.438 --> 00:00:06.619
Here's what Hammerhead does with them,

00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:07.825
in thirteen commands.

00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:12.312
Everything you're about to see runs on the labs that ship in the repo,

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:13.050
so type along.

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:17.279
Start by pointing it at a directory of configs.

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:20.597
The init command tells you what it found:

00:00:20.597 --> 00:00:22.538
three devices, nine interfaces,

00:00:22.538 --> 00:00:24.292
and three layer-three links.

00:00:25.092 --> 00:00:27.363
Check that against what you expected.

00:00:27.363 --> 00:00:29.082
If a router is missing here,

00:00:29.082 --> 00:00:31.108
every answer after this is wrong.

00:00:31.108 --> 00:00:32.704
So fix the snapshot first.

00:00:34.221 --> 00:00:36.421
Next, find out what it didn't understand.

00:00:37.146 --> 00:00:39.169
Coverage is reported per device,

00:00:39.169 --> 00:00:42.708
and this mixed-vendor lab is here to show you a bad one.

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:44.921
The Cisco core: forty-four percent.

00:00:45.821 --> 00:00:47.396
Coverage isn't accuracy.

00:00:47.396 --> 00:00:50.283
But unparsed lines are where surprises live.

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.908
So read them before you trust a verdict.

00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:57.606
Topo shows the links it inferred from the addressing.

00:00:57.606 --> 00:01:00.121
Three routers, three point-to-point links.

00:01:00.871 --> 00:01:03.691
The warnings are loopbacks with nothing on the other end,

00:01:03.691 --> 00:01:04.383
which is fine.

00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:07.169
Compare this with your own diagram.

00:01:07.169 --> 00:01:09.971
Inferred links are only as good as the configs.

00:01:11.488 --> 00:01:12.997
Now ask it a question.

00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:14.850
Can R1 reach R3's loopback?

00:01:15.662 --> 00:01:19.087
Yes. One packet, one direction. It's a start.

00:01:19.887 --> 00:01:23.111
So make it the service you actually care about.

00:01:23.111 --> 00:01:24.825
SSH, TCP port twenty-two.

00:01:25.575 --> 00:01:27.647
Traceroute gives you the hop,

00:01:27.647 --> 00:01:30.648
the egress interface, and the disposition.

00:01:30.648 --> 00:01:33.362
Delivered, out Gigabit zero slash one.

00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:36.119
And if you want to know why,

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:37.729
the forwarding table is right there.

00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:41.504
Three-dot-three, via Gig zero slash one,

00:01:41.504 --> 00:01:42.849
learned from OSPF.

00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:47.554
This is the line to compare with show IP route on the real box.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:50.618
That's the network as it is.

00:01:50.618 --> 00:01:53.092
Here's the network as someone wants it to be.

00:01:53.892 --> 00:01:57.335
Diff takes two snapshots and compares the forwarding,

00:01:57.335 --> 00:01:58.179
not the text.

00:01:58.929 --> 00:02:03.552
This candidate moves a leaf's uplinks into the wrong OSPF area.

00:02:03.552 --> 00:02:06.854
Fifteen routes affected, on all four devices.

00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:10.767
Same command, different mistake.

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:13.392
A remote AS typo on a BGP neighbor.

00:02:14.204 --> 00:02:16.344
Five routes gone, on two devices.

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:21.079
Including the two-oh-three slash sixteen you were learning from upstream.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.073
Diff tells you what changes.

00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:26.125
Preflight tells you whether to ship it.

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:29.712
It's armed by default. A blast-radius limit,

00:02:29.712 --> 00:02:32.956
and a reachability sweep between every device pair,

00:02:32.956 --> 00:02:34.037
before and after.

00:02:34.942 --> 00:02:36.166
This one fails on both.

00:02:37.066 --> 00:02:40.003
Exit code two. So your pipeline stops.

00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:44.175
Here's what a pass looks like, as JSON.

00:02:44.175 --> 00:02:46.096
A snapshot compared with itself.

00:02:46.871 --> 00:02:49.883
Read past the verdict. Gates armed: true.

00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:52.969
Three endpoints probed, of three required,

00:02:52.969 --> 00:02:54.071
and no caveats.

00:02:54.958 --> 00:02:58.313
A run with its gates switched off doesn't get to say pass.

00:02:58.313 --> 00:03:00.106
Hammerhead calls that advisory,

00:03:00.106 --> 00:03:01.783
and your pipeline should too.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.500
Some requirements shouldn't depend on anyone remembering them.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.906
So write them down. Core reachability.

00:03:09.906 --> 00:03:12.142
A path constraint. A hop budget.

00:03:12.142 --> 00:03:13.400
An isolation rule.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.258
Policy-check runs them all. Six of six here.

00:03:17.258 --> 00:03:18.742
With the strict flag,

00:03:18.742 --> 00:03:21.850
a high or critical failure is exit code two.

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:24.451
Before maintenance,

00:03:24.451 --> 00:03:26.504
ask what happens if a box goes away.

00:03:27.254 --> 00:03:30.555
Take R3 down. Four routes lost straight away,

00:03:30.555 --> 00:03:33.342
and both of the other routers feel it.

00:03:34.867 --> 00:03:37.604
Or scan every device at once, and rank them.

00:03:38.342 --> 00:03:41.161
In a triangle, they tie. On a real network,

00:03:41.161 --> 00:03:44.504
this is the list you take to the redundancy review.

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:47.446
That's thirteen commands.

00:03:48.246 --> 00:03:50.502
Keep the inputs with the change.

00:03:50.502 --> 00:03:53.251
Both snapshots, the commands, the JSON,

00:03:53.251 --> 00:03:54.308
the exit codes.

00:03:55.208 --> 00:03:57.926
Then start small. One site you know well,

00:03:57.926 --> 00:03:58.921
and one change.

00:04:00.041 --> 00:04:01.118
Break it here first.
